# This unit's real hardware configuration — the XLeRobot on its Raspberry Pi 5.
#
#   set -a && source deploy/pi/xlerobot.env.example && set +a
#   .venv/bin/python sidecar/xlerobot_sidecar.py   # then open http://<pi>:8900/control
#
# Sibling of deploy/mac/xlerobot.env.example, which carries the same unit's
# configuration as measured on the Mac Studio. Values that are host-independent
# (gripper direction, joint travel, base wiring) are copied from there; values
# marked "measured on the Pi" were re-established here and differ — see
# lex-robot#174. Do NOT copy this to another unit unexamined.
#
# Host: Raspberry Pi 5 Model B Rev 1.1, Debian aarch64, Python 3.13, lerobot 0.6.1.

LEX_ROBOT_HW=1
LEX_ROBOT_SIDECAR_PORT=8900

# ── arms ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NEVER use /dev/ttyACM* here. Linux assigns those in enumeration order, so the
# two arms swap between boots — and because the base wheels share the right
# arm's bus and the camera tower shares the left arm's bus, a swap silently
# trades the arms, the wheels, and the tower all at once. The by-id paths below
# encode the USB-serial adapters' own serial numbers and are stable across
# replug and reboot; they are the same adapters named in the mac env file
# (5B3D043715 / 5B61033220), which is what ties the two files to one robot.
#
# Observed on this Pi (2026-08-23), demonstrating the hazard is real, not
# theoretical: the numbering came up the OPPOSITE way round to the obvious
# guess — 5B3D043715 -> ttyACM1 and 5B61033220 -> ttyACM0.
#
# Which bus is which arm now rests on three independent agreeing checks:
#   1. Observed directly by the operator at the robot (2026-08-23):
#      5B61033220 is the RIGHT arm, viewed from BEHIND the robot — the same
#      viewpoint convention the mac file's gripper-state method used.
#   2. The servo IDs each bus answers on, established here without moving
#      anything: the tower's pan/tilt (7, 8 — sidecar/tower.py) sit on the
#      left arm's bus, and the base wheels (9, 10 — #145) on the right arm's.
#   3. The mac file's own assignment of these same two adapter serials.
# Three methods, one answer. Treat the left/right assignment as settled.
LEX_XLE_LEFT_PORT=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-1a86_USB_Single_Serial_5B3D043715-if00
LEX_XLE_RIGHT_PORT=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-1a86_USB_Single_Serial_5B61033220-if00

# ⚠ BUS HEALTH — intermittent, and RESEATING THE USB CLEARED IT ONCE.
#
# UPDATE, later on 2026-08-23: after the left adapter was physically unplugged
# and replugged, BOTH buses came back clean. The sidecar then brought up both
# arms — the right one had been refusing entirely minutes earlier — and 14
# consecutive status polls reported left 6/6 and right 6/6 with no failures at
# all. So the fault below is a CONTACT problem that reseating cleared, not a
# dying servo.
#
# Do not treat it as fixed. An intermittent connector that comes good when
# disturbed is the kind that returns, and the readings below are what it looks
# like when it does. If the right arm starts refusing to hand-shake again,
# reseat that bus's cabling first — and note the /display status strip shows
# "right N/6" precisely so this is visible before it wedges something.
#
# The original measurements, for comparison — read-only telemetry, 60 reads
# per servo, taken before the replug:
#
#   left bus  (5B3D043715): ids 1-8   0.0% read failures — clean
#   right bus (5B61033220): id 9     55.0% read failures  <-- base wheel
#                           id 1      6.7%
#                           id 6      6.7%
#                           ids 2,3,4,5,10  clean
#
# Failures are dropped responses, not corrupted ones: successful reads return
# stable, plausible values (spread 0 over 60 samples), and raising the serial
# timeout 5x (50 ms -> 250 ms) does not help, so this is not latency. Median
# round trip is 0.3 ms on both buses.
#
# It gets worse under camera load: with all three cameras streaming, the right
# arm's own joints (ids 1-6) go from 18.8% to 52.5% failures, while the left
# bus stays at 0.0%. Both adapters share a USB controller with two of the
# cameras, but only the right bus breaks — which points at something already
# marginal on that branch rather than at the bus budget.
#
# The operational consequence WHILE THE FAULT IS PRESENT: the sidecar cannot
# start the right arm at all. lerobot's bus handshake (_assert_motors_exist)
# pings each id once with num_retry=0, and on a bus in that state it is not
# enough — three consecutive attempts in fresh processes found {}, {4,5,6}, {}
# out of six motors, and the arm is refused with "Missing motor IDs". The left
# arm handshook cleanly throughout. After the reseat, the right arm handshakes
# first time, every time.
#
# RETESTED after the reseat, including the wheels — everything is clean:
#
#   all 16 servos, 60 reads each, cameras idle      0.0% failures
#   all 16 servos under 3x MJPG 640x480 streaming   0.0% (320 reads/bus)
#   wheel id 9 specifically                         0.0%, was 55.0%
#   right-bus handshake, 3 fresh processes          OK every time
#
# Wheel 9 also reports a position that moves by a tick between reads, so it is
# genuinely answering rather than returning a frozen value. The base bus is
# therefore trustworthy as of this measurement.
#
# The servos themselves are fine. Every one answers reads correctly once
# contact is made: `bus.connect(handshake=False)` then `ping(num_retry=3)`
# finds all six, and Present_Position reads plausible values on all of them.
# It is contact that is unreliable, not the motors.
#
# Suspect wheel 9's cable, connector or servo first, since it dominates. Until
# that is found and fixed, do not trust the base, and treat right-arm telemetry
# during multi-camera recording as lossy. Anything that reads a joint and
# silently substitutes a default on failure will read this as real motion —
# that is a live trap, not a hypothetical one.
#
# Retrying the handshake would paper over this, and it should NOT be done
# before the physical fault is found: a bus this marginal will drop commanded
# positions too, and those fail silently.

# The user must be in `dialout` to open these (XLEROBOT_SETUP.md §3.2):
#   sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER   # then log out and back in
# No other permission applies on Linux — none of macOS's TCC camera gating
# exists here, so nothing needs launching from a particular terminal app.

# Must match ~/.cache/huggingface/lerobot/calibration/robots/so_follower/<id>.json.
# Those files were NOT produced by lerobot-calibrate on this host. The
# calibration lives in the servos' own EEPROM, and `bus.read_calibration()`
# recovered it here intact — homing offsets and per-joint travel limits both
# (#174 §3). Do not re-run the interactive sweep: it would overwrite the #151
# measurements with worse ones.
LEX_XLE_LEFT_ID=xle_left
LEX_XLE_RIGHT_ID=xle_right

# Caps each commanded step. Keep it: a second line of defence under whatever
# envelope the caller enforces.
LEX_XLE_MAX_REL_TARGET=8

# ── cameras ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Indices are OpenCV indices, i.e. /dev/videoN. They are NOT the mac's values:
# each of these UVC cameras claims two /dev/video nodes on Linux (capture +
# metadata), so the three capture nodes are 0, 2 and 4 rather than 0, 1, 2.
#
# HEAD=4 was confirmed by looking at a frame from each node: node 4 shows the
# room from the tower's vantage, nodes 0 and 2 both show gripper fingers.
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_HEAD_INDEX=4

# VERIFIED on hardware 2026-08-23, by closing the LEFT gripper (id 6 on the
# left bus) and diffing each view before and after:
#
#   /dev/video2   max 32x32 block diff 249.1   25257 px changed  <-- moved
#   /dev/video0   max 32x32 block diff  14.1     301 px changed
#   /dev/video4   max 32x32 block diff  38.9    1602 px changed  (head, drift)
#
# An 18x margin over the other wrist. video2 plainly shows the two finray
# fingers closing until their tips meet; video0 is unchanged.
#
# Note this is the OPPOSITE of what enumeration order suggests, which is
# exactly the trap the mac file warns about — do not "tidy" these back into
# ascending order.
#
# Measurement gotcha, recorded because it cost real time: cameras must be
# opened AFTER the arm has moved, or with the buffer drained. V4L2 buffers
# frames, so opening a camera, moving a joint, then read()ing once returns a
# stale PRE-move frame — which reads as "the camera cannot see the gripper"
# and sends you looking for a hardware fault that is not there.
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_LEFT_INDEX=2
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_RIGHT_INDEX=0

# REQUIRED on this host, and the single most important line in this file.
#
# Measured on the Pi 2026-08-23, lerobot OpenCVCamera + PIL JPEG encode, the
# exact path the teach recorder uses. Without a fourcc, OpenCV negotiates YUYV
# (uncompressed) and the Pi's USB budget collapses:
#
#            YUYV (default)      MJPG
#   1 cam    26.1 Hz             30.0 Hz
#   2 cam    16.4 Hz             29.9 Hz
#   3 cam     9.4 Hz             30.0 Hz
#   3 cam @1280x960  fails       17.5 Hz
#
# 16.4 Hz on two cameras is below the 20 Hz the teach recorder asks for, so
# without MJPG every two-camera demonstration records short and trips
# Trajectory.validate()'s "captured at N Hz, not the requested 20" warning.
# With MJPG there is headroom to spare.
#
# This differs from the Mac in two ways worth knowing. The Mac could not set
# this fourcc at all (lerobot logged "failed to set fourcc=MJPG" and carried
# on); on this stack it takes. And the Mac's failure mode at high resolution
# was a silent one — the third camera simply returned no frame. Here the
# failure is loud: OpenCVCamera raises TimeoutError after 1000 ms. Less
# dangerous, but still a failure; keep the resolution line.
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_FOURCC=MJPG
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_WIDTH=640
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_HEIGHT=480

# Horizontal field of view, degrees. MEASURED on this unit, not a datasheet
# figure — and it matters, because every bearing scan_ahead reports is read
# off a scale drawn using this number. Wrong FOV = confidently wrong bearings,
# which is worse than none because the planner trusts them.
#
# Measured 2026-08-23 by rotating the head camera with the robot's own tower
# pan servo (id 7) and phase-correlating the resulting image shift. For a pure
# rotation, dx = f * tan(theta); fitting dx against tan(theta) over five
# angles spanning +/-10 deg gave:
#
#   focal length  386.2 px   R^2 0.9938   max residual 7 px
#   horizontal FOV  79.3 deg      vertical FOV  63.7 deg   (at 640x480)
#
# The encoder supplies the angle, so no tape measure and no human estimate is
# involved. Re-measure with sidecar/../scratch measure_fov if the lens or the
# capture resolution changes — f is in PIXELS, so it scales with frame width.
#
# Caveat worth knowing: this was measured in a nearly dark room (mean
# brightness 3/255), so phase-correlation confidence per frame was low (~0.13).
# The fit across five independent angles is what makes it trustworthy rather
# than any single frame. Re-running in daylight would tighten it.
#
# All three cameras are the same model (Sonix 05a3:9230), so this applies to
# the wrist cameras too.
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_FOV=79.3

# Why the USB budget is tight here: the Pi 5's four ports are two USB3 + two
# USB2 on a shared controller, and on this build ONE 480 Mbit/s bus carries two
# of the three cameras AND both servo adapters (lsusb -t, bus 003: two uvcvideo
# devices plus two cdc_acm). The third camera has its own bus. MJPG is what
# makes that arrangement comfortable rather than marginal.

# This unit calibrates the gripper the OPPOSITE way to the sidecar's default:
# 0 is CLOSED and 100 is OPEN. Host-independent — it is how the servo was
# calibrated, and the calibration came across in EEPROM. Without these,
# release_arm drives the gripper further shut while reporting success.
LEX_XLE_GRIPPER_OPEN_PCT=100
LEX_XLE_GRIPPER_CLOSED_PCT=0

# ── kinematics ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# placo DOES have an aarch64 Linux wheel — placo 0.9.23 ships
# manylinux_2_28_aarch64 for cp310 through cp314, so `pip install
# "lerobot[kinematics]"` resolves on the Pi with no build from source
# (#174 §4, resolved). Verified importable here on cp313.
#
# The URDF does not ship with lerobot or placo, and nothing installs it for
# you — a fresh host has placo working and still no model to load, which
# leaves move_arm's IK and the collision guard silently absent (both degrade
# quietly by design). Fetch it explicitly, out of /tmp so it survives a reboot:
#
#   git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse \
#     https://github.com/TheRobotStudio/SO-ARM100.git ~/.local/share/so-arm100
#   git -C ~/.local/share/so-arm100 sparse-checkout set Simulation
#
# Verified working on this host: RobotKinematics and InverseKinematicsEEToJoints
# both build, and /skill/read_arm_pose returned a real Cartesian pose from live
# joint angles. placo prints a "self collisions in neutral position" WARNING as
# it loads this URDF — that is normal for this model, not a fault.
LEX_XLE_URDF_PATH=${HOME}/.local/share/so-arm100/Simulation/SO101/so101_new_calib.urdf
LEX_XLE_URDF_TARGET_FRAME=gripper_frame_link

# ── base ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The wheels share the RIGHT arm's physical bus rather than having their own
# port (#145) — confirmed here: IDs 9 and 10 answer on 5B61033220.
# UNVERIFIED, carried over from the mac file: which physical wheel is ID 9 and
# which is 10 was never established. If a forward command turns the robot,
# swap these two.
LEX_XLE_BASE=diff
LEX_XLE_BASE_SHARED_ARM=right
LEX_XLE_BASE_LEFT_ID=9
LEX_XLE_BASE_RIGHT_ID=10

# ── split compute ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Pi drives; the Mac Studio sees (deploy/VISION_SPLIT.md). Set this to the
# GPU box running sidecar/vision_service.py — it is the single joining knob.
# Left unset here so the sidecar runs standalone; fill it in on the robot.
#LEX_XLE_VISION_URL=http://mac-studio.local:8901

# ── what should NOT run on this host (#174 §5) ───────────────────────────────
#   lerobot-train            — training belongs on the Mac Studio (MPS).
#   teach_to_dataset.py      — has lerobot encode camera streams to MP4; record
#                              here, copy the taught library (plain JSON +
#                              JPEGs) to the Mac, and convert there. It also
#                              needs `pip install "lerobot[dataset]"`, which is
#                              deliberately not installed on this host.
#   vision_service.py        — stays on the GPU box.

# ── not set, and why ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LEX_XLE_GRANT_PATH   defaults to manifests/xlerobot.capsule.json
# LEX_XLE_CAMERA_FPS   unset — the cameras free-run at 30 Hz with MJPG and the
#                      recorder paces itself; pinning it only adds a way to
#                      disagree with the hardware.
